<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aulus Cornelius Cossus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 437–428 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aulus Cornelius Cossus was a Roman commander of the 5th century BCE who killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lars-tolumnius">Lars Tolumnius</a>, king of Veii, in single combat during the war over Fidenae. He dedicated the king's armour in the temple of Jupiter Feretrius as the spolia opima, the second Roman after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a> said to have won this honour. He was consul in 428 BCE, and his rank at the time of the combat was debated in Augustus's day.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aulus-cornelius-cossus.jpg" alt="Aulus Cornelius Cossus" width="320" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rijksmuseum · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aulus_Cornelius_Cossus_doodt_Lars_Tolumnius_A._Cornelio_Cosso,_uccide_di_propria_mano_Larte_Tolumnio_R%C3%A9_de%27Vejenti,_e_ne_consagra_le_spoglie_opime,_al_Tempio_di_Giove_Feretrio_(titel_op_object)_Geschiedenis_van_het_Rom,_RP-P-1929-200.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The combat took place at the Battle of Fidenae in 437 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> fought Fidenae, a Roman colony in revolt, together with Falerii and Veii. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> were led by the dictator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamercus-aemilius-mamercinus">Mamercus Aemilius Mamercinus</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>'s account the cavalry officer Cossus picked out the king, unhorsed him with his spear, struck him down again with the boss of his shield and killed him, then paraded his head on a spear before the enemy, who fled.</p><p>The Senate granted Mamercinus a triumph, but Cossus was its centre. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a> in 428 BCE fell in a year of drought and plague, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a> barely flowed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> died and the government ordered the aediles to see that only the Roman gods were worshipped, in the traditional manner.</p><p>In 426 BCE Cossus was one of four consular tribunes. He held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> while the other three, Gaius Furius Pacilus Fusus, Marcus Postumius and Titus Quinctius Pennus Cincinnatus, attacked Veii and were defeated, mainly because they would not work together. When the people demanded a dictator, Cossus named Mamercinus, who made him his master of the horse.</p><p>Mamercinus recalled the army, held a public assembly and then marched on Fidenae. When a large force poured out of the city and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> wavered, the cavalry, whose horses Cossus had ordered to be ridden without bits, charged through the dust and smoke. The Romans took the enemy camp and the city and looted both.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Following the example of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a>, Cossus placed the king's sword, shield and armour beside Romulus's own spoils in the Temple of Jupiter Feretrius on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitoline Hill</a>. Only three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> ever won the spolia opima, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s highest military honour, and in the speech before the second march on Fidenae Mamercinus himself presented Cossus to the people as the man who had been awarded it.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> records a Cornelius Cossus as consul in 413 BCE without saying whether he was the same man, and this person may have been his son. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassiodorus">Cassiodorus</a> name the consul of that year as Marcus Cornelius Cossus, the son. The year is missing from the Fasti Capitolini, the list of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s highest magistrates.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aulus Cornelius Cossus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 438 BCE it went over to Lars Tolumnius of Veii and killed Roman envoys; the Roman officer <b>Aulus Cornelius Cossus</b> killed Tolumnius in single combat and dedicated his armour as the spolia opima.</blockquote><a href="/state/fidenae">Fidenae</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ancient Rome, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-433" aria-label="Ancient Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Aulus Cornelius Cossus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-433">What was here in 433 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
